    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d344">

                <pb facs="0637" n="543" sameAs="frus:frus1887/pg543"/>

                <head>No. 344.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Sir L. S. Sackville
                        West.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1887-07-19"><hi rendition="#italic">July</hi> 19,
                        1887</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge your note,
                    dated yesterday and received to-day, inclosing a copy of the declaration of
                    Captain Medes Rose, master of the schooner <hi rendition="#italic">Laura
                        Sayward</hi>, of Gloucester, Mass., made on April 12 last, at Sandy Point,
                    before a justice of peace, apparently in contradiction of the statement made by
                    the same party under oath on October 13 last.</p>
                <p>This document will be instantly made the subject of investigation, and the
                    observations of this Government thereon, as suggested by your note, will be
                    communicated to you as soon as information on the matter shall have been
                    received from the collector of customs at Gloucester, through whom the original
                    affidavits of Captain Rose were forwarded to this Department.</p>
                <p>Accept, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
